<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Hebrew copy for the widget pack.

  §0.8: *every screen is designed, and is not finished until seen in Hebrew* —
  and §4.8 records that Hebrew finds a whole defect class the English render
  cannot: a Latin-digit run inside an RTL paragraph is re-ordered by the bidi
  algorithm, so `3.2 / 4 km` and `09:00–12:00` come out backwards. That is fixed
  in code (core/util/Bidi.kt wraps every such run in an isolate pair), not here —
  a translation cannot fix it, and a translation that looks right in a review is
  exactly how it survives.

  Two things this file does NOT do, both deliberate:
  - it does not translate goal, task or life-area names. §8 puts user-authored
    content out of scope: those stay exactly as Ido typed them.
  - it does not mirror a drawn axis. The trend chart's columns are painted onto
    a bitmap and a bitmap does not flip itself; that is handled in the layout,
    which reverses the column order under an RTL layout direction.

  `Observed:` not yet rendered on a Hebrew device — the app has no language
  picker until §5.1 ships (`36-tasks-consent` is adding values-he/strings.xml as
  this lands), so this has been read but not seen. §0.8 is therefore satisfied in
  intent and not in fact, and that is recorded rather than claimed.

  ── Two wording rules, added 2026-08-16 by `widget-hebrew-terminology` (#51),
     each of which this file was violating. Same two rules as
     values-iw/analytics_strings.xml; read that file's header too.

     EIGHT of this file's resources were wrong: six on rule 1, three on rule 2,
     one on both. ──

  1 · TERMINOLOGY (§5.1 / `E1`, ratified by Ido 2026-08-16): the `Goal` entity is
      יעד, NEVER מטרה. Six resources here said מטרה, and
      gp_widget_goals_ring_meaning used both words in one sentence with the
      meanings swapped. יעד is masculine where מטרה is feminine, so the verbs
      moved with the noun (יכולה → יכול, נמצאת → עומד) — the agreement was
      wrong for as long as the noun was.

      THE HARD PART, and why this took a session: once יעד names the entity,
      Hebrew has NO second noun left for a measure's numeric target. That is not
      a gap in this app's vocabulary, it is a gap in the language as consumer
      software uses it. `Observed:` Google Fit's Hebrew help says
      "מדידה אוטומטית של ההתקרבות ליעדים" — יעד for the thing you approach, with
      no separate word for the number; and Microsoft's Hebrew Dynamics 365 uses
      both מדדי יעד and מדדי מטרה for one concept on one page
      (learn.microsoft.com/he-il/dynamics365/sales/create-edit-goal-metric),
      which is the collision showing up in a professionally localized product.
      There is nothing to borrow.

      So this file does not name the target at all: it says the ring is measured
      לפי המדד שהגדרתם לו — by the measure you set for it — which is exactly true
      (`BuildWidgetSnapshotUseCase.hasMeasure`: a measure IS the unit plus a
      positive targetValue) and is how Hebrew actually says it. Rejected:
      היעד המספרי, which reintroduces the collision this file exists to remove,
      one adjective weaker.

  2 · NO HEBREW PREFIX ATTACHED TO A LATIN OR DIGIT RUN (§4.8). `ל־%1$d` and
      `ב־%2$s` render on the FAR side of the run — the prefix lands against the
      run's last word instead of its first, the same defect as `מ‑Health Connect`
      → `Health Connect‑מ`. The remedy is a Hebrew noun or a space-separated
      preposition for the prefix to attach to, never the maqaf: `נכון לשעה %1$s`,
      `עבור %1$d מהיעדים`, `עומד על %2$s`. Three resources violated it across
      five lines: the two the brief named (as_of, goals_without_measure) and one
      it did not (effort_lead, which carries the defect twice in one string).

      THIS IS NOT THE DEFECT THE PARAGRAPH AT THE TOP OF THIS FILE DESCRIBES,
      and the two read as contradictory until you separate them. Up there, the
      run is a *value* the tile computes (`3.2 / 4 ק״מ`) and code fixes it, by
      isolating the run — a translation genuinely cannot. Here, the prefix is in
      the *template*, and `Inferred:` from what an isolate is (core/util/Bidi.kt)
      it must sit outside the isolate, so no amount of isolating the argument can
      reach it. The template is the only place this one is fixable, which is why
      it is a rule about the words and not about the code.

      grep for the class before adding a string here:
          grep -nE '[א-ת]־?%[0-9]\$' app/src/main/res/values-iw/*.xml
      HebrewTerminologyTest is the same check as a test, and covers every
      values-iw/ file rather than this one.
-->
<resources>

    <string name="gp_widget_goal">יעד</string>
    <string name="gp_widget_goals">היעדים שלכם</string>
    <string name="gp_widget_week">השבוע</string>
    <string name="gp_widget_week_large">לאן הלך השבוע</string>
    <string name="gp_widget_trend">יום אחר יום</string>
